You are a helpful assistant. Read the following passage and complete the two tasks that follow.

Passage:

The Strix Halo platform, launched by AMD in early 2025 under the Ryzen AI Max brand, marked a quiet turning point in how small teams think about local inference. Rather than pairing a conventional CPU with a discrete GPU over a narrow PCIe link, it exposes a single 96 GB pool of LPDDR5X memory, clocked at 8000 megatransfers per second, visible to both the 16 Zen-5 cores and the 40 RDNA-3.5 compute units of the integrated Radeon 8060S. The resulting 256 gigabytes per second of aggregate bandwidth is modest next to a datacenter H100, but it is the first time a 27-billion-parameter dense language model in a four-bit quantization has fit entirely inside a mini-desktop with no swapping and no discrete accelerator card. For hobbyists building private coding assistants, small-team research groups running dozens of fine-tuning experiments a week, and independent video-game studios prototyping with open-weights models, the value proposition changed: you no longer had to choose between weak local hardware and expensive cloud subscriptions with unfavorable data policies. Early benchmarks on llama.cpp with ROCm 7.2 showed decode throughput of roughly 11 tokens per second on a 27-billion-parameter Q4_K_M checkpoint, scaling to around 14 tokens per second on a 2-bit K-quant with Unsloth's dynamic scheme. Speculative decoding via the recurrent-state checkpoint infrastructure merged upstream in April 2026 pushed that number to 30 tokens per second on self-referential prompts, though performance dropped sharply on less-repetitive workloads such as fresh code generation, where the n-gram lookup cache cannot find useful matches. The platform is not a GPU replacement for frontier training, nor for serving thousands of concurrent users, but for the quiet, private, single-developer workflow it has opened a door that was previously closed unless you owned a server room.

